    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$4.35 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a full casserole with pulled pork underneath and real mashed potatoes on top. Potatoes and frozen vegetables are the cheap part, and they are most of the volume.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eRusset potatoes in the big bag make the best mash.\u003c/strong\u003e They are starchy, they whip up fluffy, and they are the least expensive potato in the store by a wide margin.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFrozen peas and canned corn are pennies a serving.\u003c/strong\u003e They add color, sweetness and bulk to the filling so a smaller amount of pork goes further.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBalsamic vinegar and Worcestershire are the depth.\u003c/strong\u003e Between them, two cheap bottles give this gravy the savory backbone that usually requires wine or hours of reduction.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eRun a fork across the top before it bakes.\u003c/strong\u003e Those ridges are what brown, and browning is flavor you get for free."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Season the pork shoulder with salt, black pepper and Italian seasoning, sear it in the olive oil, then slow cook it until it shreds and pull it apart.",
                    "Boil the peeled and quartered potatoes in salted water until they fall off a knife, then drain them well and let the steam escape for a minute.",
                    "Mash the potatoes with the butter and heavy cream, season with salt and black pepper, and stop as soon as they are smooth. Overworked potatoes turn gluey.",
                    "Cook the diced onion and sliced carrots in a skillet until soft, add the minced garlic, then sprinkle the flour over and stir for two minutes.",
                    "Add the diced tomatoes, the beef broth, the balsamic vinegar and the Worcestershire sauce and simmer until it thickens into a gravy, then fold in the pulled pork, the frozen peas and the corn.",
                    "Spread the filling in a large baking dish, spoon the mashed potatoes over the top and rake ridges into them with a fork, then bake at 400F for 30 minutes until browned and bubbling."
                ],
